  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday) \nIssued at 1011 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe first freeze of the season still looks likely for portions of\nthe region late Friday night through early Saturday morning. This\nwill be driven by conditions favorable for excellent radiational \ncooling, with clearing skies and very light winds as the surface \nridge builds directly overhead. General consensus of guidance \nstill favors lows on Saturday morning in the upper 20s over the SW\nTX Panhandle and low 30s over most of the South Plains. Should \nclouds be slower to clear than currently expected, temps may be \nable to remain above freezing, however confidence is high enough \nin temps near or just below 32F to support a Freeze Watch over the\nentire Caprock from Friday night through early Saturday morning. \nThis would be an early first freeze for this region, with the \nclimatological average first freeze for many locations still two \nor three weeks out. \n\nSurface flow will gradually obtain a stronger southerly component on \nSaturday as surface ridging shifts to our east, but will be weak \nenough to support one more very cool day with highs still in the 50s \nand low 60s Saturday afternoon. Southerly winds will become more \nrobust Sunday and beyond, which combined with the building of a \nmodest upper ridge axis will boost temperatures back towards normal \nfor this time of year next week. Models are in good agreement \nkeeping our area dry through at least Tuesday, with mid/upper \nmoisture and precipitation chances possibly returning during the \nsecond half of the week as a deep upper low settles to our west. For \nnow though, a dry forecast is still favored with poor model \nconsensus at this lead time.",
